 <div class="report-body">

 <div class="report-section" style="border-top:none;margin-top:0;">
  <div class="section-number">The objective</div>
  <div class="section-title">What This Report Measures Against</div>
  <div class="pull-quote"><div class="pq-text">&ldquo;APA members are informed, engaged, and vote.&rdquo;</div><div class="pq-src">APA Section 6 Negotiations Messaging Guide (APA_Messaging_Guide_7/27/26), July 2026</div></div>
  <div class="prose"><p>September had three moments that put APA in front of members and the public: the 25th anniversary of 9/11, Capt. Nick Silva&rsquo;s public questioning of American&rsquo;s leadership as Section 6 talks opened, and Senate passage of the pilot mental-health bills APA backed. APA&rsquo;s leaders carried the coverage, and APA&rsquo;s own channels drew their strongest response from member-life and remembrance content. The tone of public conversation about American&rsquo;s pilots and their contract improved from August.</p></div>
 </div>

 <div class="report-section">
  <div class="section-number">Scorecard</div>
  <div class="section-title">September at a Glance</div>
  <div class="data-card"><div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
   <thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>What we measure</th><th>This month</th><th>vs. last month</th><th>What it means for the objective</th></tr></thead><tbody>
   <tr><td><strong>PR</strong></td><td>News placements naming APA, Silva or Tajer</td><td>98 placements, 81 outlets; 71 name Silva or Tajer</td><td><span class="badge b-gray">First month</span></td><td>Leaders are the voice of APA&rsquo;s coverage, which members also see. Eleven placements make APA the story.</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>Social listening</strong></td><td>Tone of public talk about AA pilots, the contract and leadership</td><td>3,362 mentions; 15% negative</td><td><span class="badge b-green">Tone improved</span> from 24% negative; volume &minus;8%</td><td>Less hostile public conversation as Section 6 opens.</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>Social listening</strong></td><td>Mentions naming APA or its leaders</td><td>75</td><td><span class="badge b-amber">Down 14%</span> from 87</td><td>Small counts; more positive (19 vs. 3) but fewer overall.</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>SEO</strong></td><td>Branded search rankings and est. organic visits</td><td>All core branded terms #1; 8,921 est. visits</td><td><span class="badge b-green">Up 9%</span>; rankings flat</td><td>Members and the public searching for APA land on alliedpilots.org.</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>Social media</strong></td><td>Engagements and engagements per post</td><td>32,130 engagements on 34 posts</td><td><span class="badge b-gray">First month</span></td><td>Member-life content engages most; Section 6 posts sit mid-pack.</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>Poll</strong></td><td>Member survey (UNH)</td><td>Sept 2025 baseline: 81% say APA is good value; 58% rate BOD communication good</td><td><span class="badge b-gray">No new wave</span></td><td>Members value APA but feel less informed. BOD communication fell from 77% in Feb 2025. October&rsquo;s poll will show whether that has moved.</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>Website</strong></td><td>GA4 sessions, engagement, key events</td><td>Access pending</td><td><span class="badge b-gray">&mdash;</span></td><td>Needed to see whether coverage and posts drive members to APA&rsquo;s site.</td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
   <div class="note">APA hasn&rsquo;t set numeric targets for these measures, so the scorecard reports direction only. Most areas start their month-over-month comparison in October.</div>
  </div>
 </div>

 <div class="report-section">
  <div class="section-number">Pillar check</div>
  <div class="section-title">How the Messaging Pillars Showed Up</div>
  <div class="data-card"><div class="tbl-wrap"><table>
   <thead><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>September evidence</th><th>Where to look</th></tr></thead><tbody>
   <tr><td><strong>1 &mdash; Masters of Our Own Destiny</strong></td><td>Capt. Silva is named in 36 news placements and Capt. Tajer in 35, including <em>Forbes</em>, <em>The Dallas Morning News</em> and a Reuters story on the Senate bills.</td><td>PR</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>2 &mdash; Talking With You, Not At You</strong></td><td>Member-life posts drew about 1,950 engagements per post, the most of any theme. The new-hire dinners post hit a 58.7% engagement rate, and &ldquo;Meet the Negotiators&rdquo; opened at 36.1% on Facebook.</td><td>Social media</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>3 &mdash; Accountability, Not Defensiveness</strong></td><td>Little measurable activity. The Sept 1 town hall clips, which include a 12-minute Aggressive Pick-Up segment, were the only APU content. Monitoring found almost no public APU discussion.</td><td>Social media, PR</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>4 &mdash; They Deserve Better</strong></td><td>No measurable activity this period. No flying-public messaging ran in September.</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr>
   <tr><td><strong>5 &mdash; If You&rsquo;re Not at the Table, You&rsquo;re on the Menu</strong></td><td>The leadership critique reached business and aviation press. Headlines framed it sharply (&ldquo;blasts CEO&rsquo;s leadership,&rdquo; &ldquo;calling for a new CEO&rdquo;), and 18 of 21 placements scored negative. No analyst or investor-outlet coverage turned up, and share of voice vs. ALPA isn&rsquo;t measured yet.</td><td>PR</td></tr>
   </tbody></table></div>
  </div>
 </div>

 <div class="report-section">
  <div class="section-number">Watch list</div>
  <div class="section-title">Risks and Opportunities</div>
  <div class="split">
   <div class="data-card risk"><div class="dc-title">Worth watching</div>
    <div class="risk-item"><strong>Headline framing.</strong> The guide calls for a media tone that is &ldquo;reasoned and methodical, not combative.&rdquo; Coverage of the Sept 16 comments leaned the other way in its headlines.</div>
    <div class="risk-item"><strong>The contract as a competitive issue.</strong> Two pieces argued the pilot contract is costing American in Austin, a framing that may resurface during talks.</div>
    <div class="risk-item"><strong>Political giving.</strong> The APA PAC appeared in a story about donors to lawmakers who made remarks about Islam.</div>
    <div class="risk-item"><strong>Pilot conduct stories.</strong> A viral jet bridge confrontation identified an American pilot by his APA lanyard.</div>
   </div>
   <div class="data-card opp"><div class="dc-title">Opportunities</div>
    <div class="risk-item"><strong>The Senate mental-health win.</strong> Capt. Silva&rsquo;s quote ran in 32 placements, but APA didn&rsquo;t post about the bills on Facebook, Instagram or LinkedIn in September.</div>
    <div class="risk-item"><strong>Member-life content.</strong> It is APA&rsquo;s most engaging material and a natural carrier for Section 6 updates.</div>
    <div class="risk-item"><strong>October poll.</strong> This is the first read since the September 2025 baseline, when only 58% rated Board communication good, down from 77%. Repeating that question will show whether Section 6 communication is closing the gap.</div>
   </div>
  </div>
 </div>

 <div class="report-section">
  <div class="section-number">Recommendations</div>
  <div class="section-title">What the Data Supports</div>
  <div class="rec-list">
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">01</div><div><div class="rec-title">Share the Senate bill win with members</div><div class="rec-text">The mental-health bills APA supported passed the Senate with Capt. Silva quoted in 32 placements, yet no APA post on Facebook, Instagram or LinkedIn covered them. A member-facing post or video message would turn earned coverage into member information.</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">02</div><div><div class="rec-title">Pair Section 6 updates with member-life content</div><div class="rec-text">Member-life posts drew about three times the engagement per post of Section 6 posts (1,952 vs. 648). Keep &ldquo;Meet the Negotiators&rdquo; running, and place negotiation updates alongside the events and people members respond to.</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">03</div><div><div class="rec-title">Give Pillar 3 (APU) a visible moment</div><div class="rec-text">Apart from the Sept 1 town hall clips, the accountability pillar had no measurable activity. A short update on APU fixes would give members something concrete, which the guide calls &ldquo;actions speak louder than words.&rdquo;</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">04</div><div><div class="rec-title">Check the leadership message against the guide&rsquo;s media tone</div><div class="rec-text">Before the next statement on American&rsquo;s leadership, weigh how September&rsquo;s headlines read against the guide&rsquo;s &ldquo;reasoned, not combative&rdquo; standard and its advice to let third parties carry the strongest lines.</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">05</div><div><div class="rec-title">Tighten monitoring before October</div><div class="rec-text">Most of what four Meltwater searches return is noise. For example, 212 of 214 &ldquo;APU&rdquo; results were unrelated. There is no ALPA volume search, and the listening topic still catches World War II &ldquo;Allied pilots.&rdquo; Fixing these makes next month&rsquo;s trends reliable.</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">06</div><div><div class="rec-title">Close the data gaps</div><div class="rec-text">Connect GA4 and Search Console, share the 2025 poll raw data and the October questionnaire, and have IT review the test sites appearing in Google (SEO tab).</div></div></div>
