 <div class="section-number">Poll Results</div>
 <div class="section-title">Member Survey &mdash; September 2025 Baseline</div>
 <div class="source-line">UNH Survey Center &mdash; &ldquo;APA Survey September 2025 &mdash; Dashboard&rdquo; &mdash; fielded Sept 2&ndash;16, 2025 &mdash; 901 completed interviews &mdash; 79% response rate</div>
 <div class="prose"><p>This is the most recent member survey. No new wave was fielded in September 2026, so these results are shown as the baseline for the UNH poll expected in October. Figures are UNH&rsquo;s own, as printed on the dashboard slides, and question wording is quoted as UNH wrote it. UNH&rsquo;s respondent profile closely matches the full membership by age, base, division, equipment, seniority and seat.</p></div>
 <div class="kpi-strip">
  <div class="kpi"><div class="kpi-label">Say APA is good value</div><div class="kpi-value">81%</div><div class="kpi-sub">Up from 77% (Feb 2025) and 75% (Jan 2024)</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="kpi-label">Overall view of APA positive</div><div class="kpi-value">51%</div><div class="kpi-sub">Up from 48% (Feb 2025), down from 60% (Jan 2024)</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="kpi-label">Rate BOD communication good</div><div class="kpi-value">58%</div><div class="kpi-sub">Down from 77% in Feb 2025</div></div>
  <div class="kpi"><div class="kpi-label">Scheduling rules #1 priority</div><div class="kpi-value">60%</div><div class="kpi-sub">80% rank it first or second</div></div>
 </div>

 <div class="report-section">
  <div class="section-number">Takeaways</div>
  <div class="section-title">What the Survey Tells Us</div>
  <div class="rec-list">
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">01</div><div><div class="rec-title">Members value APA, but feel less informed by the Board</div><div class="rec-text">81% said APA&rsquo;s services and representation are a very good or good value. But only 58% rated the Board of Directors&rsquo; communication and keeping members informed as very good or good, down 19 points from 77% in February 2025. That rating fell in every age, base, seat and seniority group UNH reported. It is the survey&rsquo;s most direct measure of the &ldquo;informed&rdquo; half of APA&rsquo;s objective.</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">02</div><div><div class="rec-title">Overall sentiment is recovering but hasn&rsquo;t returned to 2024 levels</div><div class="rec-text">51% viewed APA very positively or positively, up from 48% in February 2025 but below 60% in January 2024. 18% were negative. Among negative respondents, the leading reasons were poor contract negotiations (23%), &ldquo;corruption and self-serving leadership&rdquo; (19%) and lack of representation and listening to members (15%). Among positive respondents, the top reason was the union&rsquo;s focus on pilot interests (21%).</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">03</div><div><div class="rec-title">Scheduling, not pay rates, is the top contract priority</div><div class="rec-text">60% named scheduling rules as the most important thing to improve in bargaining, and 80% ranked it first or second. Compensation was first for 18% (46% first or second). Within scheduling, improved pairing quality led at 47%. Within pay, trip and duty rigs led at 38% (61% first or second), ahead of hourly rates (26%).</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">04</div><div><div class="rec-title">Members want a better deal over a faster one</div><div class="rec-text">55% disagreed that it is better to reach a deal sooner with fewer changes (29% agreed). 73% disagreed with securing a contract first so APA can re-enter talks sooner &ldquo;even if it means we set the market,&rdquo; and 61% agreed it is better to let other carriers ratify first. Junior pilots hold this view most strongly: 76% of 31-to-40-year-olds rejected a faster, smaller deal, compared with 36% of pilots 61 and older.</div></div></div>
   <div class="rec-item"><div class="rec-num">05</div><div><div class="rec-title">Support for &ldquo;spreading the wealth&rdquo; on premium flying is rising</div><div class="rec-text">64% agreed with scheduling changes that would &ldquo;spread the wealth,&rdquo; such as Seniority in Turn for Premium, up from 49% in September 2024. Agreement that the most senior pilots should always get first opportunity at premium sequences slipped from 59% to 53%. This is the fault line behind Aggressive Pick-Up, and it splits by seniority.</div></div></div>
  </div>
 </div>

